{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqb3vxazrub","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Grader self-runs via opportunistic pressure nudge like the judge, not the nightly cron","summary":"Decision (2026-06-12, user-confirmed): the gate labeler/grader (scripts/gate-label.js) is integrated via the SAME opportunistic pressure-nudge mechanism as the self-running judge — NOT the nightly QA scheduled task. Rationale: the 2AM cron is fragile (needs the desktop app open, can stall on permission prompts, fails silently). The judge pattern is robust: a /judge/pressure endpoint + an hourly-throttled UserPromptSubmit-hook nudge dispatches ONE background drain pass off LIVE session activity, claim-attributed to followup/harness-judge-drain. Mirror it for the grader: new /label/pressure endpoint (nudge:true when gradable backlog — UNLABELED journal rows whose task_key is now TERMINAL — crosses a threshold, 1/hour throttle) + a parallel [Grader] block in hooks/classify.sh that dispatches a background grader pass claiming followup/harness-label-drain. Opportunistic is strictly better here, not a workaround: if there is no live activity there is also no new data being created, and labels are not time-sensitive (training is batched weeks out), so 'drain when active' loses nothing while the cron fails during dead time. The grader is DETERMINISTIC (pure script, no LLM), so the dispatched pass is trivially cheap (just runs the script + reports counts) — even lighter than the judge, can use a cheap model. This AMENDS the integration mechanism in [[note-mqb1ic5y3qr]] (which had said 'labeler in nightly QA'); the rest of that phase plan stands. Opportunistic path SUPERSEDES nightly for the grader.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-12T15:53:27.131Z","vec":[-0.05729859694838524,0.09484226256608963,-0.05101762339472771,-0.05323784053325653,-0.008467612788081169,-0.058373309671878815,0.030470481142401695,0.020020727068185806,0.007336627691984177,-0.010639570653438568,0.02786633